(1367,'thunderfl thunderca @b13-dwarfs @b17-922 @matterhorn','Big Thunder is the gold-mine train attraction at both named parks.',['Both official park pages identify Big Thunder.','Seven Dwarfs is the Snow White jewel-mine coaster, not the southwestern gold-mine attraction.','Everest is the Animal Kingdom attraction, not the gold-mine ride in these two parks.','Matterhorn is Disneyland\'s bobsled-run attraction, not a gold-mine train.'],'The question requires the same named attraction at both parks and southwestern gold-mine theming. Additional source notes distinguish each other ride\'s theme or park.'),
(1368,'tokyo tokyocor','Tokyo Disneyland was the first overseas Disney theme park, in 1983.',['The operator explicitly establishes both the first and the year.','Disneyland Paris is not the first established by the operator\'s chronology.','Hong Kong Disneyland is not that 1983 first park.','Shanghai Disneyland is not the operator\'s identified first.'],'Corroborated the first claim through OLC and D23 rather than inferring first from the Tokyo date alone. This is a theme park, not any Disney business presence abroad.'),
(1372,'lillian','Walt married Lillian in 1925.',['The Disney biography names Lillian Bounds.','Ruth does not match the bride\'s name.','Flora does not match the bride\'s name.','Edna is not the named bride.'],'The question asks first name, not the maiden surname tested in #929. Removed the unnecessary lifelong-by-his-side characterization.'),
(1376,'mary','Mary Blair\'s visual style defines the original attraction.',['Disney explicitly attributes its style and concept to Mary Blair.','Earle is not the artist given this defining style credit.','Marc Davis is not the person credited for this overarching color style.','Hench is not the artist named for this defining style.'],'Other artists contributed to small world; the revised question asks the defining visual style rather than implying sole creation of all designs. The institutional attribution is explicit.'),
(1377,'weenie weeniecor','Walt used weenie/wienie for inviting visual landmarks.',['The design history and Hench quotation identify this term.','Anchor is generic vocabulary, not the whimsical term identified.','Icon describes a landmark but is not the quoted Walt term.','Focal point is descriptive, not the identified whimsical term.'],'Literal generic synonyms could describe the same object, so the stem specifically asks Walt\'s whimsical vocabulary. It does not demand proving he invented landmark design.'),
(1378,'plus','Walt\'s term for elevating an experience is plussing.',['Disney explicitly attributes plussing to Walt.','Upgrading is a generic near-synonym, not the attributed single term.','Imagineering names the design discipline, not this improvement term.','Sprucing is not the term identified in the Disney explanation.'],'Near-synonyms describe improvement, but the question asks the particular word attributed to Walt, supported directly by the institutional passage.'),
(1379,'medal','Johnson presented Walt the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 1964.',['The dated ceremony title and Walter Disney citation establish this honor.','National Medal of Arts is not the award at this ceremony.','Congressional Gold Medal is not the medal presented in this presidential ceremony.','Liberty Medal is not the identified award.'],'Walt\'s other honors do not fit this named presenter and year. Removed presidential wording from the stem so the title does not give itself away.'),
